# No catch-all. Every published address is a real file, so a miss must be a real 404 — the
# `/* /index.html 200` this replaces answered every typo on the domain with the home page, which a
# search engine indexes as content (a soft 404) and a reader mistakes for the page they asked for.
#
# Pages serves the root 404.html for a miss on its own; these two rules only pick the shell that
# matches the language tree the address was under, so a wrong link under /pt-br explains itself in
# Portuguese. Status 404 (not 200), which is also why naming an .html destination is safe here:
# Pages normalizes extensionless destinations on 200 rewrites only.
# Portuguese moved from /pt-br to /pt on Sep 6 2026 — the hreflang TAG still says pt-BR, only the
# address changed. A 301 rather than nothing: those addresses were published, and dropping them
# turns each into a 404. Permanent, so a crawler moves the credit across instead of keeping two
# spellings of one page. These come first, because Pages takes the FIRST match.
/pt-br     /pt          301
/pt-br/*   /pt/:splat   301

/pt/*      /pt/404.html      404
/es/*      /es/404.html      404
